[7.27955, "o", "\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[7.311475, "o", "\"\"\"pygrep: a GNU grep 3.8 replacement in pure Python.\r\n\r\nUsage: python3 /app/pygrep/grep.py [OPTION]... PATTERNS [FILE]...\r\n\"\"\"\r\n\r\nimport sys\r\n\r\n\r\n"]
[7.311799, "o", "def main(argv):\r\n    sys.stderr.write(\"pygrep: not implemented yet\\n\")\r\n    return 2\r\n\r\n\r\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\r\n    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))\r\n"]
[7.311964, "o", "\u001b[?2004h"]
[7.312009, "o", "root@165c5e029d76:/app# "]
[7.499234, "i", "sed -n '1,220p' /app/docs/grep.txt\n"]
[7.499749, "o", "sed -n '1,220p' /app/docs/grep.txt"]
[7.499804, "o", "\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[7.508963, "o", "1 Introduction\r\n"]
[7.518692, "o", "**************\r\n\r\nGiven one or more patterns, ‘grep’ searches input files for matches to\r\nthe patterns.  When it finds a match in a line, it copies the line to\r\nstandard output (by default), or produces whatever other sort of output\r\nyou have requested with options.\r\n\r\n   Though ‘grep’ expects to do the matching on text, it has no limits on\r\ninput line length other than available memory, and it can match\r\narbitrary characters within a line.  If the final byte of an input file\r\nis not a newline, ‘grep’ silently supplies one.  Since newline is also a\r\nseparator for the list of patterns, there is no way to match newline\r\ncharacters in a text.\r\n\r\n2 Invoking ‘grep’\r\n*****************\r\n\r\nThe general synopsis of the ‘grep’ command line is\r\n\r\n     grep [OPTION...] [PATTERNS] [FILE...]\r\n\r\nThere can be zero or more OPTION arguments, and zero or more FILE\r\narguments.  The PATTERNS argument contains one or more patterns\r\nseparated by newlines, and is omitted when patterns are given via the\r\n"]
[7.519117, "o", "‘-e PATTERNS’ or ‘-f FILE’ options.  Typically PATTERNS should be quoted\r\n"]
[7.519234, "o", "when ‘grep’ is used in a shell command.\r\n"]
[7.519372, "o", "\r\n"]
[7.519455, "o", "* Menu:\r\n"]
[7.519588, "o", "\r\n"]
[7.519657, "o", "* Command-line Options::        Short and long names, grouped by category.\r\n"]
[7.51969, "o", "* Environment Variables::       POSIX, GNU generic, and GNU grep specific.\r\n"]
[7.519729, "o", "* Exit Status::                 Exit status returned by ‘grep’.\r\n"]
[7.519759, "o", "* grep Programs::               ‘grep’ programs.\r\n"]
[7.519788, "o", "\r\n"]
[7.519818, "o", "2.1 Command-line Options\r\n"]
[7.519845, "o", "========================\r\n"]
[7.519871, "o", "\r\n"]
[7.519899, "o", "‘grep’ comes with a rich set of options: some from POSIX and some being\r\n"]
[7.519928, "o", "GNU extensions.  Long option names are always a GNU extension, even for\r\n"]
[7.519957, "o", "options that are from POSIX specifications.  Options that are specified\r\n"]
[7.519984, "o", "by POSIX, under their short names, are explicitly marked as such to\r\n"]
[7.52002, "o", "facilitate POSIX-portable programming.  A few option names are provided\r\n"]
[7.52006, "o", "for compatibility with older or more exotic implementations.\r\n"]
[7.520095, "o", "\r\n"]
[7.520124, "o", "* Menu:\r\n"]
[7.520149, "o", "\r\n"]
[7.520175, "o", "* Generic Program Information::\r\n"]
[7.520202, "o", "* Matching Control::\r\n"]
[7.520234, "o", "* General Output Control::\r\n"]
[7.520303, "o", "* Output Line Prefix Control::\r\n"]
[7.520355, "o", "* Context Line Control::\r\n"]
[7.520392, "o", "* File and Directory Selection::\r\n"]
[7.520417, "o", "* Other Options::\r\n"]
[7.520443, "o", "\r\n"]
[7.52047, "o", "   Several additional options control which variant of the ‘grep’\r\n"]
[7.520692, "o", "matching engine is used.  *Note grep Programs::.\r\n"]
[7.520858, "o", "\r\n"]
[7.520937, "o", "2.1.1 Generic Program Information\r\n"]
[7.521012, "o", "---------------------------------\r\n"]
[7.521083, "o", "\r\n"]
[7.521167, "o", "‘--help’\r\n"]
[7.521242, "o", "     Print a usage message briefly summarizing the command-line options\r\n"]
[7.521357, "o", "     and the bug-reporting address, then exit.\r\n"]
[7.521433, "o", "\r\n"]
[7.521507, "o", "‘-V’\r\n"]
[7.521586, "o", "‘--version’\r\n"]
[7.521667, "o", "     Print the version number of ‘grep’ to the standard output stream.\r\n"]
[7.521751, "o", "     This version number should be included in all bug reports.\r\n"]
[7.521828, "o", "\r\n"]
[7.521954, "o", "2.1.2 Matching Control\r\n"]
[7.522035, "o", "----------------------\r\n"]
[7.522104, "o", "\r\n"]
[7.522219, "o", "‘-e PATTERNS’\r\n"]
[7.522254, "o", "‘--regexp=PATTERNS’\r\n"]
[7.522282, "o", "     Use PATTERNS as one or more patterns; newlines within PATTERNS\r\n"]
[7.522313, "o", "     separate each pattern from the next.  If this option is used\r\n"]
[7.522346, "o", "     multiple times or is combined with the ‘-f’ (‘--file’) option,\r\n"]
[7.522374, "o", "     search for all patterns given.  Typically PATTERNS should be quoted\r\n"]
[7.522409, "o", "     when ‘grep’ is used in a shell command.  (‘-e’ is specified by\r\n"]
[7.522452, "o", "     POSIX.)\r\n"]
[7.522484, "o", "\r\n"]
[7.522511, "o", "‘-f FILE’\r\n"]
[7.522538, "o", "‘--file=FILE’\r\n"]
[7.522565, "o", "     Obtain patterns from FILE, one per line.  If this option is used\r\n"]
[7.522593, "o", "     multiple times or is combined with the ‘-e’ (‘--regexp’) option,\r\n"]
[7.522619, "o", "     search for all patterns given.  The empty file contains zero\r\n"]
[7.522647, "o", "     patterns, and therefore matches nothing.  (‘-f’ is specified by\r\n"]
[7.522676, "o", "     POSIX.)\r\n"]
[7.522701, "o", "\r\n"]
[7.522727, "o", "‘-i’\r\n"]
[7.522776, "o", "‘-y’\r\n"]
[7.522818, "o", "‘--ignore-case’\r\n"]
[7.522845, "o", "     Ignore case distinctions in patterns and input data, so that\r\n"]
[7.522872, "o", "     characters that differ only in case match each other.  Although\r\n"]
[7.522899, "o", "     this is straightforward when letters differ in case only via\r\n"]
[7.522926, "o", "     lowercase-uppercase pairs, the behavior is unspecified in other\r\n"]
[7.522954, "o", "     situations.  For example, uppercase “S” has an unusual lowercase\r\n"]
[7.522982, "o", "     counterpart “ſ” (Unicode character U+017F, LATIN SMALL LETTER LONG\r\n"]
[7.523025, "o", "     S) in many locales, and it is unspecified whether this unusual\r\n"]
[7.523055, "o", "     character matches “S” or “s” even though uppercasing it yields “S”.\r\n"]
[7.523092, "o", "     Another example: the lowercase German letter “ß” (U+00DF, LATIN\r\n"]
[7.523167, "o", "     SMALL LETTER SHARP S) is normally capitalized as the two-character\r\n"]
[7.523198, "o", "     string “SS” but it does not match “SS”, and it might not match the\r\n"]
[7.523226, "o", "     uppercase letter “ẞ” (U+1E9E, LATIN CAPITAL LETTER SHARP S) even\r\n"]
[7.523257, "o", "     though lowercasing the latter yields the former.\r\n"]
[7.523284, "o", "\r\n"]
[7.523311, "o", "     ‘-y’ is an obsolete synonym that is provided for compatibility.\r\n"]
[7.523341, "o", "     (‘-i’ is specified by POSIX.)\r\n"]
[7.523367, "o", "\r\n"]
[7.523407, "o", "‘--no-ignore-case’\r\n"]
[7.523454, "o", "     Do not ignore case distinctions in patterns and input data.  This\r\n"]
[7.523483, "o", "     is the default.  This option is useful for passing to shell scripts\r\n"]
[7.523511, "o", "     that already use ‘-i’, in order to cancel its effects because the\r\n"]
[7.523537, "o", "     two options override each other.\r\n"]
[7.523562, "o", "\r\n"]
[7.523591, "o", "‘-v’\r\n"]
[7.523617, "o", "‘--invert-match’\r\n"]
[7.523645, "o", "     Invert the sense of matching, to select non-matching lines.  (‘-v’\r\n"]
[7.52368, "o", "     is specified by POSIX.)\r\n"]
[7.523727, "o", "\r\n"]
[7.523762, "o", "‘-w’\r\n"]
[7.523797, "o", "‘--word-regexp’\r\n"]
[7.523849, "o", "     Select only those lines containing matches that form whole words.\r\n"]
[7.523893, "o", "     The test is that the matching substring must either be at the\r\n"]
[7.523919, "o", "     beginning of the line, or preceded by a non-word constituent\r\n"]
[7.523948, "o", "     character.  Similarly, it must be either at the end of the line or\r\n"]
[7.523974, "o", "     followed by a non-word constituent character.  Word constituent\r\n"]
[7.524001, "o", "     characters are letters, digits, and the underscore.  This option\r\n"]
[7.524035, "o", "     has no effect if ‘-x’ is also specified.\r\n"]
[7.52406, "o", "\r\n"]
[7.524088, "o", "     Because the ‘-w’ option can match a substring that does not begin\r\n"]
[7.524115, "o", "     and end with word constituents, it differs from surrounding a\r\n"]
[7.524144, "o", "     regular expression with ‘\\<’ and ‘\\>’.  For example, although ‘grep\r\n"]
[7.524174, "o", "     -w @’ matches a line containing only ‘@’, ‘grep '\\<@\\>'’ cannot\r\n"]
[7.524201, "o", "     match any line because ‘@’ is not a word constituent.  *Note\r\n"]
[7.524228, "o", "     Special Backslash Expressions::.\r\n"]
[7.52426, "o", "\r\n"]
[7.524317, "o", "‘-x’\r\n"]
[7.524375, "o", "‘--line-regexp’\r\n"]
[7.524403, "o", "     Select only those matches that exactly match the whole line.  For\r\n"]
[7.52443, "o", "     regular expression patterns, this is like parenthesizing each\r\n"]
[7.524475, "o", "     pattern and then surrounding it with ‘^’ and ‘$’.  (‘-x’ is\r\n"]
[7.524502, "o", "     specified by POSIX.)\r\n"]
[7.524526, "o", "\r\n"]
[7.524552, "o", "2.1.3 General Output Control\r\n"]
[7.524623, "o", "----------------------------\r\n"]
[7.52466, "o", "\r\n"]
[7.524684, "o", "‘-c’\r\n"]
[7.52471, "o", "‘--count’\r\n"]
[7.524734, "o", "     Suppress normal output; instead print a count of matching lines for\r\n"]
[7.52476, "o", "     each input file.  With the ‘-v’ (‘--invert-match’) option, count\r\n"]
[7.524785, "o", "     non-matching lines.  (‘-c’ is specified by POSIX.)\r\n"]
[7.524809, "o", "\r\n"]
[7.524833, "o", "‘--color[=WHEN]’\r\n"]
[7.524858, "o", "‘--colour[=WHEN]’\r\n"]
[7.524883, "o", "     Surround matched non-empty strings, matching lines, context lines,\r\n"]
[7.524908, "o", "     file names, line numbers, byte offsets, and separators (for fields\r\n"]
[7.524932, "o", "     and groups of context lines) with escape sequences to display them\r\n"]
[7.524956, "o", "     in color on the terminal.  The colors are defined by the\r\n"]
[7.524982, "o", "     environment variable ‘GREP_COLORS’ and default to\r\n"]
[7.525007, "o", "     ‘ms=01;31:mc=01;31:sl=:cx=:fn=35:ln=32:bn=32:se=36’ for bold red\r\n"]
[7.525034, "o", "     matched text, magenta file names, green line numbers, green byte\r\n"]
[7.525059, "o", "     offsets, cyan separators, and default terminal colors otherwise.\r\n"]
[7.525083, "o", "     *Note Environment Variables::.\r\n"]
[7.525105, "o", "\r\n"]
[7.525177, "o", "     WHEN is ‘always’ to use colors, ‘never’ to not use colors, or\r\n"]
[7.525211, "o", "     ‘auto’ to use colors if standard output is associated with a\r\n"]
[7.525237, "o", "     terminal device and the ‘TERM’ environment variable’s value\r\n"]
[7.525284, "o", "     suggests that the terminal supports colors.  Plain ‘--color’ is\r\n"]
[7.525321, "o", "     treated like ‘--color=auto’; if no ‘--color’ option is given, the\r\n"]
[7.525368, "o", "     default is ‘--color=never’.\r\n"]
[7.525393, "o", "\r\n"]
[7.525427, "o", "‘-L’\r\n"]
[7.525458, "o", "‘--files-without-match’\r\n"]
[7.525491, "o", "     Suppress normal output; instead print the name of each input file\r\n"]
[7.525516, "o", "     from which no output would normally have been printed.\r\n"]
[7.525538, "o", "\r\n"]
[7.525568, "o", "‘-l’\r\n"]
[7.52564, "o", "‘--files-with-matches’\r\n"]
[7.525806, "o", "     Suppress normal output; instead print the name of each input file\r\n     from which output would normally have been printed.  Scanning each\r\n     input file stops upon first match.  (‘-l’ is specified by POSIX.)\r\n\r\n‘-m NUM’\r\n‘--max-count=NUM’\r\n     Stop after the first NUM selected lines.  If NUM is zero, ‘grep’\r\n     stops right away without reading input.  A NUM of −1 is treated as\r\n     infinity and ‘grep’ does not stop; this is the default.\r\n\r\n     If the input is standard input from a regular file, and NUM\r\n     selected lines are output, ‘grep’ ensures that the standard input\r\n     is positioned just after the last selected line before exiting,\r\n     regardless of the presence of trailing context lines.  This enables\r\n     a calling process to resume a search.  For example, the following\r\n     shell script makes use of it:\r\n\r\n          while grep -m 1 'PATTERN'\r\n          do\r\n            echo xxxx\r\n          done < FILE\r\n\r\n     But the following probably will not work becau"]
[7.525861, "o", "se a pipe is not a\r\n     regular file:\r\n\r\n          # This probably will not work.\r\n          cat FILE |\r\n          while grep -m 1 'PATTERN'\r\n          do\r\n            echo xxxx\r\n          done\r\n\r\n     When ‘grep’ stops after NUM selected lines, it outputs any trailing\r\n     context lines.  When the ‘-c’ or ‘--count’ option is also used,\r\n     ‘grep’ does not output a count greater than NUM.  When the ‘-v’ or\r\n     ‘--invert-match’ option is also used, ‘grep’ stops after outputting\r\n     NUM non-matching lines.\r\n\r\n‘-o’\r\n‘--only-matching’\r\n"]
[7.531544, "o", "\u001b[?2004hroot@165c5e029d76:/app# "]
[62.372307, "i", "cat > /app/pygrep/grep.py <<'PY'\r#!/usr/bin/env python3\r\"\"\"Small, byte-oriented GNU grep 3.8 compatible implementation.\"\"\"\rimport os\rimport re\rimport sys\r\r\rdef err(s):\r    sys.stderr.write(\"grep: \" + s + \"\\n\")\r\r\rdef split_patterns(s, file_source=False):\r    a = s.split('\\n')\r    if file_source and s.endswith('\\n'):\r        a.pop()\r    return a\r\r\rdef translate_class(c):\r    classes = {\r        '[:alnum:]': 'A-Za-z0-9', '[:alpha:]': 'A-Za-z',\r        '[:blank:]': ' \\\\t', '[:cntrl:]': '\\\\x00-\\\\x1f\\\\x7f',\r        '[:digit:]': '0-9', '[:graph:]': '\\\\x21-\\\\x7e',\r        '[:lower:]': 'a-z', '[:print:]': '\\\\x20-\\\\x7e',\r        '[:punct:]': '!-/:-@[-`{-~', '[:space:]': '\\\\t-\\\\r ',\r        '[:upper:]': 'A-Z', '[:xdigit:]': 'A-Fa-f0-9'\r    }\r    for k, v in classes.items():\r        c = c.replace(k, v)\r    return c\r\r\rdef trans_regex(p, mode):\r    \"\"\"Translate common GNU/POSIX ERE or BRE syntax to Python's ASCII re.\"\"\"\r    out = []\r    i = 0\r    inclass = False\r    while i < len(p):\r        ch = p[i]\r        if ch == '[' "]
[62.379808, "o", "cat > /app/pygrep/grep.py <<'PY'\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> #!/usr/bin/env python3\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> \"\"\"Small, byte-oriented GNU grep 3.8 compatible implementation.\"\"\"\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> import os\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> import re\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> import sys\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> def err(s):\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h>     sys.stderr.write(\"grep: \" + s + \"\\n\")\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> def split_patterns(s, file_source=False):"]
